amethyst (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Methods: `impl P { fn m(self: P, ...) -> T { ... } }` declared in
    impl blocks (several per struct are merged), called as `p.m(...)` with
    self bound to the receiver; type, arity and argument checks match the
    ones for plain functions.
  * String concatenation: `+` joins two strings and `+=` appends, both
    returning a heap string kept until the program exits (still no
    collector).
  * Dynamic arrays grow: `var a: int[] = [];` starts empty, `push(a, v)`
    doubles the capacity when the block fills (0 -> 4 -> 8 -> ...) and
    `pop(a)` returns and removes the last element (runtime error on an
    empty array). push/pop only accept a local array, never a fixed-size
    one or a slice parameter.
  * Math builtins: sqrt, abs, min, max. Together with len, int, float,
    push and pop they are reserved names: a user function can no longer
    redefine them nor start with `__amethyst_`.
  * float[] and string[] slice parameters parse again (regression fixed).
  * New examples: math.amt, concat.amt, dynarray.amt, methods.amt.
  * VS Code extension (vscode-amethyst/): .amt syntax highlighting,
    snippets and Compile / Compile and Run commands. Its sources are
    installed under /usr/share/amethyst/vscode/; the ready-to-install
    amethyst-1.3.0.vsix ships as a separate asset of the GitHub release.
  * Documentation website updated for methods, dynamic arrays, string
    concatenation and the builtin functions.

 -- Amethyst Project <amethyst@localhost>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0000

amethyst (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * struct type: declarations `struct P { x: int, y: float }` with
    comma-separated fields, read and written as `p.x`, and usable before
    being declared (`struct Node { next: Node }`).
  * Objects live on the heap: `var p = new P { x: 1, y: 2 };` takes every
    field exactly once in any order, `var p: P = null;` holds no object
    yet, and reading a field of null stops the program with a runtime
    error. Structs are comparable only against null.
  * `free(p);` releases an object or a `new` array and nulls the
    variable, so a second free is a no-op. One free per object: there is
    still no garbage collector, and aliases of a freed object dangle.
  * Arrays of every element type: int, bool, float, string and structs,
    in fixed `T[N]` form, as slices `T[]` and in array literals.
  * Dynamic arrays: `var a: int[] = new int[n];` — n is any int
    expression read once, the block starts zeroed, the length lives with
    the pointer and drives the bounds check, and `free(a)` resets it to 0.
  * New keywords: struct, new, free, null. `new T[n]` may only initialize
    a `T[]` variable; a fixed-size array or an array parameter cannot be
    freed.
  * Runtime errors added: null reference, out of memory and array size
    must be positive.
  * New examples: structs.amt, heap.amt; documentation website updated
    for structs and heap arrays.

 -- Amethyst Project <amethyst@localhost>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0000

amethyst (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Compound assignment (+= -= *= /= %=), len() builtin and a
    division/modulo-by-zero guard that exits with a runtime error.
  * Compiler warnings: unused variable and unreachable code, reported
    on stderr without stopping the build.
  * Array parameters passed as slices: int[] / bool[] (pointer + length),
    dynamic bounds checks and for i in 0..len(a).
  * string type: variables, parameters and returns, == / != compare
    contents, len() via strlen, print via puts.
  * float type: 64-bit IEEE 754 binary64 literals and arithmetic on SSE
    registers, explicit float(n) / int(x) conversions, print with %.15g.
  * Definite-assignment analysis: `var x: T;` may be left unassigned
    until every path that reads it has assigned it.
  * New examples: slices.amt, strings.amt, floats.amt, definite.amt.
  * Dev Kit Homepage now points at the actual repository.

 -- Amethyst Project <amethyst@localhost>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0000

amethyst (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Dev Kit release.
  * Ships amethystc (x86-64 GAS backend), amethyst-new helper,
    examples, templates, README and man pages.
  * Depends on gcc + binutils so beginners never build the compiler by hand.

